```java
// Insert 99 at index 1 of an array-backed list: shift everything right
int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int[] b = new int[a.length + 1];
b[0] = a[0];
b[1] = 99;
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) b[i + 1] = a[i];  // O(n) shift
// b = {1, 99, 2, 3, 4}
```

```java
class Node { int val; Node next; Node(int v){ val = v; } }

// Insert 99 after a node we already hold: just relink pointers
void insertAfter(Node node, int value) {
    Node fresh = new Node(value);
    fresh.next = node.next;   // new node points to the rest
    node.next = fresh;        // previous node points to new node
}                             // O(1) - nothing shifts
```
The O(1) advantage assumes you **already have the node reference**; finding it first is still O(n).

